  rankdir="TB";
  "IM11" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-Case: Currently space-bandwidth product determines depth of focus and modulators only modulate intensity."];
  "IM18" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-Case: Currently Algorithms exist to generate phase patterns and GS is a method to calculate them."];
  "IM2" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-Reasoning: Photopolymerization can be volumetric, and volumetric printing eliminates support struts, so Volumetric AM enables complex designs without supports."];
  "IM20" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-Case: Currently Demo prints mm-scale objects and achieves less than 60s time with 31um features."];
  "IM4" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-Case: Currently TVAM solidifies objects simultaneously and is layer-less."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) HoloVAM proposes a phase-encoded tomographic volumetric additive-manufacturing approach in which Radon-based projection patterns are converted into phase holograms for a digital micromirror device using Lee holography and Gerchberg–Saxton optimization, aiming to form depth-controlled, speckle-suppressed 3D light doses for rapid fabrication of millimeter-scale polymer and cell-laden hydrogel objects with approximately 30 µm features using a low-power 40 mW diode-laser light engine.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) Replacing intensity-only projected amplitude patterns with computable phase-encoded holographic projections can address optical-efficiency and propagation-control limitations while preserving the volumetric, layer-less advantages of tomographic VAM."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) Imaging a two-dimensional spatial light modulator into a cylindrical vial can produce blurring artifacts when the vial diameter is much larger than the depth of focus."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) The SLM-imaged tomographic VAM configuration has a conditional depth-of-focus blurring risk, and its intensity-only modulation does not provide phase control."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) Phase encoding of tomographic projections offers an efficiency advantage over amplitude modulation because all pixels of the display can contribute."];
  "R16" [label="(0,0,0) Phase encoding can encode point-spread-function modification, and a self-healing beam enables printing within scattering materials."];
  "R17" [label="(0,0,0) Phase encoding offers multiple optical advantages for tomographic projection, including improved display-pixel contribution and the ability to encode PSF-related beam modifications."];
  "R19" [label="(0,0,0) Phase encoding is a feasible strategy for tomographic projection because it offers optical advantages and established methods such as Gerchberg–Saxton can calculate two-dimensional input phase patterns."];
  "R21" [label="(0,0,0) The reported short printing time is attributed to the efficiency of the light engine, consistent with efforts to improve light-dose delivery to the photoresin."];
  "R22" [label="(0,0,0) The HoloVAM demonstration reports millimeter-scale printing in under 60 seconds with reliable 31.35 µm negative features, and attributes the short printing time to light-engine efficiency."];
  "R23" [label="(0,0,0) Together, the feasibility of phase-pattern generation and the reported millimeter-scale, sub-minute, fine-feature demonstration support HoloVAM as a promising phase-encoded tomographic VAM implementation."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Tomographic VAM implementations rely on Radon-transform-computed light amplitude patterns, and digital micromirror devices are typically used as reflective binary amplitude modulators."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) The Radon transform assumes straight rays for computing projected light patterns, and projection patterns can be regarded as extruded two-dimensional patterns propagating as a collimated beam."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Tomographic VAM commonly combines Radon-computed projection patterns with a straight-ray or collimated-beam optical model, often implemented with DMD amplitude modulation."];
  "S10" [label="(10,0,0) Implementations of tomographic VAM rely on the Radon transform to compute the light amplitude patterns."];
  "S11" [label="(11,0,0) Essentially, different techniques have been used to improve the light dose delivered to the photoresin."];
  "S17" [label="(17,0,0) The Radon transform assumes straight rays to compute the projected light patterns."];
  "S18" [label="(18,0,0) Projection patterns can be thought of as extruded two-dimensional patterns that propagate as a collimated beam."];
  "S19" [label="(19,0,0) In practice, this is implemented by imaging a two-dimensional spatial light modulator into a cylindrical vial."];
  "S20" [label="(20,0,0) If the vial's diameter is much larger than the depth of focus, blurring artifacts appear."];
  "S21" [label="(21,0,0) The space-bandwidth product of the light source is a key optical parameter, as it determines the depth of focus."];
  "S23" [label="(23,0,0) Additionally, in this type of optical imaging configuration, the two-dimensional spatial light modulator modulates only the intensity, not the phase."];
  "S24" [label="(24,0,0) Typically, digital micromirror devices (DMD) are used as reflective binary amplitude modulators."];
  "S28" [label="(28,0,0) Phase encoding of tomographic projections offers multiple advantages over amplitude modulation."];
  "S29" [label="(29,0,0) First, phase-encoding improves light efficiency, as all pixels of the display can contribute."];
  "S3" [label="(3,0,0) Photopolymerization can be produced sequentially and more recently also volumetrically."];
  "S32" [label="(32,0,0) Second, phase-encoding allows a modification of the point spread function (PSF) to be encoded."];
  "S33" [label="(33,0,0) A self-healing beam enables printing within scattering materials."];
  "S36" [label="(36,0,0) There are multiple iterative algorithms or machine learning approaches that can generate two-dimensional input phase patterns."];
  "S37" [label="(37,0,0) The Gerchberg–Saxton (GS) iterative algorithm is one of the many methods to calculate a two-dimensional input phase pattern."];
  "S39" [label="(39,0,0) In our approach, we demonstrate the use of phase encoding in tomographic VAM by printing millimeter-scale objects."];
  "S4" [label="(4,0,0) By printing in a volumetric fashion, support struts are no longer needed."];
  "S40" [label="(40,0,0) We demonstrate a printing time of less than 60 s and reach negative features (holes) of 31.35 μm reliably."];
  "S41" [label="(41,0,0) This short printing time is the result of the efficiency of the light engine."];
  "S5" [label="(5,0,0) In TVAM, an entire three-dimensional object is simultaneously solidified."];
  "S6" [label="(6,0,0) Unlike most other additive manufacturing methods, TVAM is layer-less."];
